.SS "int32_t schultz_a11y_backend_pump (\fBschultz_a11y_backend\fP * backend)"

.PP
Gives the platform a moment to talk, on platforms that need one\&. Linux accessibility is a D-Bus connection the application owns, so somebody has to read it; the frame driver calls this every frame\&. Nothing else works that way, and everywhere else this does nothing\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIbackend\fP The backend\&. NULL is accepted and does nothing\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_a11y_backend_set_window (\fBschultz_a11y_backend\fP * backend, \fBschultz_rect\fP window)"

.PP
Tells the platform where the window is\&. Only platforms that publish a window in their own right need this\&. Where accessibility hangs off a view, the view already knows where it is, and this does nothing\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIbackend\fP The backend\&. NULL is accepted and does nothing\&. 
.br
\fIwindow\fP The window rectangle in screen pixels\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_a11y_backend_set_focused (\fBschultz_a11y_backend\fP * backend, int32_t focused)"

.PP
Tells the platform whether the window has focus\&. This is the outbound half of focus: whether the application is the one the user is typing into\&. It is not where a screen reader's cursor is, which travels the other way and arrives as an action request\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIbackend\fP The backend\&. NULL is accepted and does nothing\&. 
.br
\fIfocused\fP Nonzero when the window has focus\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK\&. 
.RE
.PP

